{
 "id": "M3-c138",
 "lesson": "L08",
 "front": "If c is not 0 or 1, and column j of the data is multiplied by c where M_jl is nonzero for some l other than j, can the new matrix M' be a multiple of M?",
 "back": "No. The diagonal entry M_jj is multiplied by c^2 and the off-diagonal entries in row and column j by c, so no single number kappa gives M' = kappa M (Remark 2.4). For c = 1 nothing changes.",
 "grade": "T"
}
{
 "id": "M3-c141",
 "lesson": "L08",
 "front": "In Lab 1, what does multiplying the 0.25-year column by ten do to the first explained share?",
 "back": "It moves the first explained share, a share of the total over all six eigenvalues, from 0.7587 to 0.9791 on the SIMULATED panel (Lab 1 output). This is one case, and no general rule for such a change is given.",
 "grade": "C",
 "basis": "Lab 1, one SIMULATED six-tenor panel, one case"
}
{
 "id": "M3-c145",
 "lesson": "L08",
 "front": "Misconception: the first principal component is always the level factor, because yield curves move in parallel. Does the lesson support this claim?",
 "back": "The lesson does not support it. The components are ordered by their eigenvalues (Definition 1.3), and Theorem 2.1 concerns the squared movement along each direction, not which shape a direction has. In the SIMULATED Lab 1 panel, PC1 is level-like, and the generator planted the largest scale, 8 bp per day, on the level factor. The lab does not vary the planted scales, so one panel does not show what sets the order or that the first component is level-like in general. Nothing here shows that real yield curves move in parallel.",
 "grade": "C",
 "basis": "Theorem 2.1 and the Lab 1 panel (one SIMULATED case)"
}
{
 "id": "M3-c174",
